[Unit]
Description=Restart ride-watch (triggered by ride-watch-restart.path)
Documentation=file:///home/rwt/projects/otp-minneapolis/ride-watch/README.md

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# Never mid-ride. A restart is safe in the ways that matter — KillMode=process
# leaves the rider's live tmux ride thread alone, the trip is re-adopted on the
# next UPDATE_PROGRESS, and the report deadline survives in state.json — but it
# is not free: per-trip rule state (stall anchors, notification windows,
# destination convergence, the 2-page budget) resets, and the adopted trip
# spawns a SECOND ride thread the rider then sees in their app. So the file
# changing during a ride is not a reason to interrupt it; the digest's own
# STALE line tells the ride thread what it is running, and the human decides.
#
# Never mid-WRAP-UP either, which is what this condition did not ask until
# 2026-09-22 (24.1). On 2026-09-21 16:43:53 the 16:05 ride had ended sixty
# seconds earlier and its console was writing the report; "^No active trip"
# was perfectly true, so the restart went ahead, took the console away, opened
# the finished 16:31 sub-ride out of the START_GO_MODE records still in the
# stream, filed four findings about a rider already home, spawned a second
# console and paged "Ride ended — 5 findings. Report pending" 25 s before the
# real report landed. The comment above already said "the human decides";
# this unit was deciding.
#
# Both questions now live in restart-ok.sh next to this file, because a
# condition written inline here cannot use shell variables — systemd expands
# $NAME itself and leaves the empty string behind. Missing or unreadable
# status file still means no restart: do not act on a state you cannot
# observe. The daemon stamps each pending wrap-up with its own expiry, so a
# process that dies inside a window cannot block restarts for ever.
ExecCondition=/home/rwt/projects/otp-minneapolis/ride-watch/restart-ok.sh
ExecStart=/usr/bin/systemctl --user restart ride-watch.service
